{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"flowered-creased-serpent","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nSibling sweep of bug 5546 (rebar-debug Step 7). 5546's root cause is a repeatable\npattern, not a one-off: **a bare `git fetch <remote> <branch>` whose result is then\nconsumed as `<remote>/<branch>`.** A bare fetch always writes FETCH_HEAD but only\n*opportunistically* writes `refs/remotes/<remote>/<branch>` — and only when the remote's\nCONFIGURED refspec covers that branch. Under a narrow refspec\n(`+refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main`, which is what `git clone --single-branch`\nAND `actions/checkout` produce by default) the ref is never written, or — worse, when it\nalready exists — never UPDATED, so the consumer silently reads a missing or STALE ref.\n\n5546 fixed the `sync.py` instance. Two live instances remain; both were confirmed\nsemantically (read in context), not by string match.\n\n## Sibling 1 — `src/rebar/_store/push.py:182`\n\n```python\n_git(base_path, \"fetch\", remote, branch)          # :182  bare fetch\n...\nmerge = _git(base_path, \"merge\", remote_ref, ...) # :202  consumes f\"{remote}/{branch}\"\n```\n\nThis is the non-fast-forward retry loop. Under a narrow refspec the merge target is\nmissing or stale, so the merge cannot absorb what actually rejected the push and **every\nretry re-pushes the same rejected history until the budget is exhausted.**\n\n## Sibling 2 — `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:286,309` (and the canary at :424,:436)\n\n```sh\ngit fetch origin tickets || true                      # :286\nahead=$(git rev-list --count origin/tickets..HEAD ...) # :287  consumes origin/tickets\n...\ngit fetch origin tickets 2>&1 || ...                  # :309  inside the retry loop\n```\n\nNote the SAME workflow already uses the correct explicit form at line 115\n(`git fetch origin '+tickets:refs/remotes/origin/tickets'`), as does\n`verify-identity.yml:55` — so these sites are internally inconsistent, which is good\nevidence they are an oversight rather than a deliberate choice.\n\n**This is a strong candidate root cause for bug 449f-f9bf-be90-47fe** (and for the\nReconcile Bridge push-rejection evidence recorded in 5546's own comment thread: 4 runs on\n2026-07-30 exhausting a 5-retry budget with `! [rejected] HEAD -> tickets (fetch first)`).\nBecause line 115 creates `refs/remotes/origin/tickets` earlier in the job, the later bare\nfetches at :286/:309 leave it STALE rather than absent — so `rev-list origin/tickets..HEAD`\nand the reconcile merge both operate on a snapshot that never advances, and the retry loop\nprovably cannot converge no matter how large the budget. That mechanism has NOT yet been\nexperimentally confirmed against a real runner; it is a cited hypothesis, and confirming or\nrejecting it is the first task here.\n\n## Deliberately NOT siblings (checked and cleared)\n\n- `src/rebar/opcert_service/workspace.py:70,87` — bare fetch, then consumes\n  `review/<branch>` / `tickets/<branch>`. Same shape, but the workspace is built by\n  `git init` + `git remote add`, which installs the DEFAULT WILDCARD refspec, so the\n  branch is always covered. Not reachable.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py:347`, `review_bot/gerrit_client.py:335`,\n  `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:115`, `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:86`,\n  `verify-identity.yml:55` — already use an explicit refspec. Correct as written.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/init.py:375` — a genuine instance, but bug 01e8-302b-ab8f-4220\n  already owns and fixes it. Excluded to avoid a conflicting edit.\n\n## Why filed rather than folded into 5546\n\n5546's authorized surface was `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` + its tests. `push.py` and the\nCI workflows are separate blast radii — the workflow change in particular needs a real\nrunner to verify — so they get their own reviewable change rather than riding along.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The 449f hypothesis above is experimentally confirmed or rejected against a real\n      narrow-refspec checkout; if confirmed, 449f is linked to this ticket.\n- [ ] `push.py`'s retry-loop fetch uses an explicit refspec, with a test proving the\n      non-fast-forward retry loop converges under a narrow clone refspec (it currently\n      cannot).\n- [ ] The reconcile-bridge workflows' bare `git fetch origin tickets` sites use the\n      explicit refspec already used at line 115 of the same file.\n- [ ] A guard (lint rule, test, or reviewer-facing note) prevents the bare-fetch form\n      from being reintroduced.","id":"35f7-a39d-8e27-4922","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["sync","store","ci","data-integrity"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"sibling sweep of 5546: bare git fetch leaves <remote>/<branch> missing or stale in push.py and the reconcile-bridge workflows"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785517434165163001,"uuid":"226b5e7e-4f48-4bc6-b1f7-cf111b61723c"}